1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,600
You've seen that one before?

2
00:00:01,600 --> 00:00:04,040
All right, so isn't that, at least that kind of just seems

3
00:00:04,040 --> 00:00:04,800
true by definition.

4
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:07,400
How could anything be otherwise?

5
00:00:07,400 --> 00:00:07,900
All right.

6
00:00:11,120 --> 00:00:13,720
But we're actually going to see even that stuff you can test.

7
00:00:13,720 --> 00:00:17,200
But in any case, if we assume the preferences are stable,

8
00:00:17,200 --> 00:00:18,780
preferences are stable, they don't just

9
00:00:18,780 --> 00:00:20,840
jump around all the time.

10
00:00:20,840 --> 00:00:23,440
And this is almost always done in empirical work.

11
00:00:23,440 --> 00:00:24,840
When economists do empirical work,

12
00:00:24,840 --> 00:00:27,920
they almost always assume stable preferences.

13
00:00:27,920 --> 00:00:31,040
In that case, we get a lot of testable assumptions.

14
00:00:31,040 --> 00:00:34,280
Why would economists assume stable preferences?

15
00:00:34,280 --> 00:00:43,840
Suppose that the supply goes up and the price goes up.

16
00:00:43,840 --> 00:00:47,560
And then you were just to say, well, what must have happened

17
00:00:47,560 --> 00:00:51,600
was that the preferences of consumers changed.

18
00:00:51,600 --> 00:00:55,240
And so demand went up and went off at the same time, too.

19
00:00:55,240 --> 00:00:56,840
We have no actual evidence of this.

20
00:00:56,840 --> 00:00:58,520
You just say it.

21
00:00:58,520 --> 00:01:00,860
This would not seem very satisfying to most economists,

22
00:01:00,860 --> 00:01:02,960
because it seems like it's a theory that

23
00:01:02,960 --> 00:01:05,720
explains whatever happens.

24
00:01:05,720 --> 00:01:09,680
And theories that explain all conceivable events,

25
00:01:09,680 --> 00:01:10,960
what do we call those?

26
00:01:10,960 --> 00:01:11,840
Unfalse survival.

27
00:01:11,840 --> 00:01:13,720
Unfalse survival, or tautologies,

28
00:01:13,720 --> 00:01:16,880
or crummy theories.

29
00:01:16,880 --> 00:01:18,240
It's like, wait a second.

30
00:01:18,240 --> 00:01:20,200
If capitalism does well, then you

31
00:01:20,200 --> 00:01:21,920
say that it's exploiting the third world.

32
00:01:21,920 --> 00:01:23,360
And as poorly, you say that it's collapsing

33
00:01:23,360 --> 00:01:25,080
under its internal congregations.

34
00:01:25,080 --> 00:01:28,200
So what are the observations that shows that you're wrong?

35
00:01:28,200 --> 00:01:30,760
There are no observations that show I'm wrong,

36
00:01:30,760 --> 00:01:32,920
because I'm right.

37
00:01:32,920 --> 00:01:34,680
There is a little bit of pushback to this.

38
00:01:34,680 --> 00:01:37,920
So Fisher Black had the line, the easiest theory

39
00:01:37,920 --> 00:01:41,000
to falsify is one that's false.

40
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:45,200
But still, there is a lot of resistance

41
00:01:45,200 --> 00:01:47,840
to unfalsifiable theories.

42
00:01:47,840 --> 00:01:50,320
And then furthermore, if we go on

43
00:01:50,320 --> 00:01:52,080
and assume that preferences are selfish

44
00:01:52,080 --> 00:01:55,160
in the ordinary language sense of the word,

45
00:01:55,160 --> 00:01:57,680
and this is also a very common auxiliary assumption

46
00:01:57,680 --> 00:01:59,560
for economists to use.

47
00:01:59,560 --> 00:02:03,360
In like, if demand for wheat goes up,

48
00:02:03,360 --> 00:02:06,120
we normally think that customers are going to pay more.

49
00:02:06,120 --> 00:02:08,200
But imagine someone would say, well,

50
00:02:08,200 --> 00:02:12,040
but since wheat farmers love their fellow men,

51
00:02:12,040 --> 00:02:14,240
when they find out that other people need more food,

52
00:02:14,240 --> 00:02:16,320
they'll work harder to go and grow them more food.

53
00:02:16,320 --> 00:02:20,120
And so maybe the price won't go up at all.

54
00:02:20,120 --> 00:02:25,120
And in terms of logical possibility, is that wrong?

55
00:02:25,120 --> 00:02:29,000
Yeah, you know, imagine that the consumer is my son,

56
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:30,400
and I'm the farm owner.

57
00:02:30,400 --> 00:02:31,880
And then my son wants more food.

58
00:02:31,880 --> 00:02:33,840
Do I go and check out the price for my kid?

59
00:02:33,840 --> 00:02:36,800
So now for now, dinner costs $10, kid.

60
00:02:36,800 --> 00:02:38,640
No, normally if my kids get hungrier,

61
00:02:38,640 --> 00:02:40,880
I just get more food and give it to them.

62
00:02:40,880 --> 00:02:46,040
So that's what it's like to have a market where the sellers love

63
00:02:46,040 --> 00:02:51,440
the buyers so much that the normal functioning doesn't

64
00:02:51,440 --> 00:02:53,080
really hold anymore.

65
00:02:53,080 --> 00:02:56,160
So lurking in the background of our story about markets

66
00:02:56,160 --> 00:02:58,720
is the very plausible assumption that wheat farmers don't

67
00:02:58,720 --> 00:03:04,160
love you, and that they're just growing wheat for the money.

68
00:03:04,160 --> 00:03:06,040
Can you believe this?

69
00:03:06,040 --> 00:03:08,560
You ever thought about how little the wheat farmer loves

70
00:03:08,560 --> 00:03:09,200
you?

71
00:03:09,200 --> 00:03:10,720
You're going to get a bag of flour.

72
00:03:10,720 --> 00:03:11,880
And so he's going to buy it.

73
00:03:11,880 --> 00:03:13,080
And I thought he loved me.

74
00:03:13,080 --> 00:03:15,000
But now, when I just realized he's just

75
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:17,200
selling me flour for money, for what?

76
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:21,880
And then when we expand things further

77
00:03:21,880 --> 00:03:25,520
to expected utility theory, expected utility theory

78
00:03:25,520 --> 00:03:28,960
adds on a bunch of further empirical implications.

79
00:03:28,960 --> 00:03:35,080
Things like there will be a linear effect of a risk going

80
00:03:35,080 --> 00:03:38,280
from 0 to 1, or 1 to 0, or 1 to 2.

81
00:03:38,280 --> 00:03:40,520
So remember, one implication of expected utility theory

82
00:03:40,520 --> 00:03:42,720
is people won't pay a special premium

83
00:03:42,720 --> 00:03:45,160
to drive risk down to 0.

84
00:03:45,160 --> 00:03:48,200
They'll pay the same amount to go from 2 to 1

85
00:03:48,200 --> 00:03:50,160
as they'll go from 1 to 0.

86
00:03:50,160 --> 00:03:51,040
And that's testable.

87
00:03:51,040 --> 00:03:54,360
And you probably have an intuition that's wrong, right?

88
00:03:54,360 --> 00:03:57,240
And that intuition is very likely very true.

89
00:04:00,040 --> 00:04:03,320
So there's a big literature at this point, which strangely

90
00:04:03,320 --> 00:04:06,280
is usually called behavioral economics, which

91
00:04:06,280 --> 00:04:10,120
does all the tests that other economists,

92
00:04:10,120 --> 00:04:12,800
both economists usually don't bother doing.

93
00:04:12,800 --> 00:04:14,560
Again, why behavioral economics?

94
00:04:14,560 --> 00:04:15,720
What kind of a name is that?

95
00:04:15,720 --> 00:04:16,880
It's a stupid name, right?

96
00:04:16,880 --> 00:04:18,880
It's like, well, all economics is about behavior.

97
00:04:18,880 --> 00:04:20,920
So what are you talking?

98
00:04:20,920 --> 00:04:23,080
Anyway, this is another one of those cases

99
00:04:23,080 --> 00:04:27,440
where you would either fight an entire profession for a word,

100
00:04:27,440 --> 00:04:30,440
or you can just get with the program and use the same word

101
00:04:30,440 --> 00:04:31,840
in the same way as everyone else,

102
00:04:31,840 --> 00:04:33,200
and everyone understands you.

103
00:04:33,200 --> 00:04:34,880
And then the only drawback is when you teach it

104
00:04:34,880 --> 00:04:38,640
to someone else, you have to give this thing up.

105
00:04:38,640 --> 00:04:40,400
But again, it would have been much better

106
00:04:40,400 --> 00:04:42,960
if it had been called psychological economics.

107
00:04:42,960 --> 00:04:46,120
Or sometimes it is just called psychology and economics,

108
00:04:46,120 --> 00:04:47,160
something like that.

109
00:04:47,160 --> 00:04:49,080
Or it could have been called that.

110
00:04:49,080 --> 00:04:50,400
And those are alternate ones.

111
00:04:50,400 --> 00:04:53,560
But behavioral econ is the name that it's primarily used.

112
00:04:53,560 --> 00:04:55,920
So that's the one we're going to be talking about.

113
00:04:55,920 --> 00:04:59,480
It's basically psychologically informed economics.

114
00:05:02,560 --> 00:05:05,920
So behavioral economics goes and does empirical tests

115
00:05:05,920 --> 00:05:08,000
that most economists just don't bother doing,

116
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:10,920
because they think they know how they're going to turn out.

117
00:05:10,920 --> 00:05:13,680
Or they just think of them as so true by definition,

118
00:05:13,680 --> 00:05:15,760
how could they be wrong?

119
00:05:15,760 --> 00:05:18,560
And the product of behavioral economics

120
00:05:18,560 --> 00:05:23,200
is anomalies, results that are wrong.

121
00:05:23,200 --> 00:05:26,040
Or namely, things that are the empirical results that

122
00:05:26,040 --> 00:05:28,600
seem to congregate very simple economic models.

123
00:05:31,120 --> 00:05:33,240
And of course, one thing you can do

124
00:05:33,240 --> 00:05:34,720
is try to make the anomaly go away

125
00:05:34,720 --> 00:05:36,360
and say, well, it looks like anomaly actually

126
00:05:36,360 --> 00:05:37,120
really makes sense.

127
00:05:37,120 --> 00:05:40,080
It can be explained totally by normal micro theory.

128
00:05:40,080 --> 00:05:42,920
Another thing you can do is, no, the facts are solid.

129
00:05:42,920 --> 00:05:44,200
The theory's wrong.

130
00:05:44,200 --> 00:05:47,240
You can do that too.

131
00:05:47,240 --> 00:05:48,880
And by the way, who is the economist

132
00:05:48,880 --> 00:05:51,080
who famously had a column in the Journal of Economics

133
00:05:51,080 --> 00:05:52,600
called Anomalies?

134
00:05:52,600 --> 00:05:54,760
Richard Naylor.

135
00:05:54,760 --> 00:05:56,440
And at least some people are suggesting

136
00:05:56,440 --> 00:05:59,600
he's the only economist to ever get a Nobel Prize for a column.

137
00:05:59,600 --> 00:06:05,920
I think that is, there's a chance that's right, actually.

138
00:06:05,920 --> 00:06:07,400
So what you normally would do with the columns

139
00:06:07,400 --> 00:06:09,000
is you talk about his own research

140
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:10,200
and research of other people.

141
00:06:10,200 --> 00:06:12,160
Sometimes it would only be about other people

142
00:06:12,160 --> 00:06:13,280
if he hadn't been involved.

143
00:06:13,280 --> 00:06:15,480
But he did have his finger in, say, many pies.

144
00:06:15,480 --> 00:06:17,400
And so if you could go and mention that he'd

145
00:06:17,400 --> 00:06:19,400
done something about it, you could mention that,

146
00:06:19,400 --> 00:06:21,960
but only other people as well.

147
00:06:21,960 --> 00:06:23,840
So this is a long-running column.

148
00:06:23,840 --> 00:06:25,680
We're just explaining to other economists

149
00:06:25,680 --> 00:06:27,520
there's some facts that don't fit the theory.

150
00:06:27,520 --> 00:06:31,000
All right, does anyone have questions about that?

151
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:33,000
OK.

152
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:34,920
And again, of course, to be a real anomaly,

153
00:06:34,920 --> 00:06:36,680
you want the evidence to be robust.

154
00:06:36,680 --> 00:06:39,480
You don't want it to just be one little experiment that

155
00:06:39,480 --> 00:06:40,560
found something weird.

156
00:06:40,560 --> 00:06:43,160
You want it to be a whole pile of evidence,

157
00:06:43,160 --> 00:06:44,400
a stack of evidence.

158
00:06:44,400 --> 00:06:46,360
Like, how do you explain this?

159
00:06:46,360 --> 00:06:47,760
Look at this stuff.

160
00:06:47,760 --> 00:06:51,000
Like 1,000 pages of results.

161
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:52,760
All right.

162
00:06:52,760 --> 00:06:54,560
And again, so this is a long-running column.

163
00:06:54,560 --> 00:06:59,680
All right, so starting off with this evidence

164
00:06:59,680 --> 00:07:02,880
that people are sometimes violating very basic axioms

165
00:07:02,880 --> 00:07:04,440
of choice theory.

166
00:07:04,440 --> 00:07:06,360
Now, do behavioral economists claim

167
00:07:06,360 --> 00:07:08,000
that all regular economics is wrong?

168
00:07:10,520 --> 00:07:12,080
No, they don't.

169
00:07:12,080 --> 00:07:15,920
And actually, if you read the original and others closely,

170
00:07:15,920 --> 00:07:17,920
normally they are happy to admit, well, sure,

171
00:07:17,920 --> 00:07:20,320
the theory explains all this other stuff.

172
00:07:20,320 --> 00:07:23,200
And the naive implications of someone

173
00:07:23,200 --> 00:07:25,680
might want to draw a wrong.

174
00:07:25,680 --> 00:07:28,280
So for example, if you go to read Robert Shiller

175
00:07:28,280 --> 00:07:31,160
in his Irrational Exuberance, a Nobel Prize winner,

176
00:07:31,160 --> 00:07:34,800
famous for his behavioral economics and finance,

177
00:07:34,800 --> 00:07:37,680
does he claim that he's got some great list of investing

178
00:07:37,680 --> 00:07:41,720
strategies that you can use to get super rich really easily?

179
00:07:41,720 --> 00:07:43,600
No, he doesn't claim any of that.

180
00:07:43,600 --> 00:07:45,600
Does he claim that he can forecast

181
00:07:45,600 --> 00:07:48,920
what the market's going to do with any kind of accuracy?

182
00:07:48,920 --> 00:07:50,760
No, he doesn't.

183
00:07:50,760 --> 00:07:51,760
Wall Street hates him.

184
00:07:51,760 --> 00:07:54,600
And see how he won so much money with this one simple trick.

185
00:07:54,600 --> 00:07:55,100
Yeah.

186
00:07:55,100 --> 00:07:59,120
Is that a real thing, or is that just a lie?

187
00:07:59,120 --> 00:08:02,480
I was just thinking of that one, like the click bait.

188
00:08:02,480 --> 00:08:06,440
Yeah, that is a good click bait.

189
00:08:06,440 --> 00:08:08,000
Anyway, if you go and read the books,

190
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:08,880
the books are not click bait.

191
00:08:08,880 --> 00:08:10,200
They're usually kind of boring.

192
00:08:10,200 --> 00:08:13,960
And they usually contain admissions that,

193
00:08:13,960 --> 00:08:16,400
like the anomaly is often very small,

194
00:08:16,400 --> 00:08:19,680
or it's one where you might have to go and live for 1,000 years

195
00:08:19,680 --> 00:08:22,520
to have a 40% chance, or let's say,

196
00:08:22,520 --> 00:08:25,000
55% chance of making money off of it.

197
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:26,920
So normally, when you actually read it,

198
00:08:26,920 --> 00:08:31,680
you will find out their claims are fairly tame.

199
00:08:31,680 --> 00:08:35,480
Although, when it's popularized, the popularizations often

200
00:08:35,480 --> 00:08:36,360
go wild.

201
00:08:36,360 --> 00:08:37,920
It's like, this is the guy who showed

202
00:08:37,920 --> 00:08:40,040
that all neoclassical economics is completely wrong

203
00:08:40,040 --> 00:08:41,200
and worthless.

204
00:08:41,200 --> 00:08:43,800
And occasionally, the authors themselves,

205
00:08:43,800 --> 00:08:46,720
when they give the two-sentence summary of their work,

206
00:08:46,720 --> 00:08:49,120
make it, or actually, when you listen to that,

207
00:08:49,120 --> 00:08:51,360
sometimes they actually are guilty of challenging

208
00:08:51,360 --> 00:08:53,440
the popularizers with their work here.

209
00:08:53,440 --> 00:08:56,280
So anyway, if you read the real work,

210
00:08:56,280 --> 00:09:00,600
then what they're saying is not particularly shocking.

211
00:09:00,600 --> 00:09:03,400
All right, so all of these violation of choice theorem,

212
00:09:03,400 --> 00:09:05,560
those who are going to have choice theory, sometimes

213
00:09:05,560 --> 00:09:06,880
called irrationality.

214
00:09:10,720 --> 00:09:12,520
Sometimes called irrationality.

215
00:09:12,520 --> 00:09:15,600
And again, especially if you're an economist who got your PhD

216
00:09:15,600 --> 00:09:18,160
before the rational expectations revolution.

217
00:09:18,160 --> 00:09:21,360
So if you're an economist who got your PhD before the 70s,

218
00:09:21,360 --> 00:09:25,320
then you're likely to use the word irrationality in this way.

219
00:09:25,320 --> 00:09:28,360
You violate basic axioms, that means rationality.

220
00:09:28,360 --> 00:09:31,800
Now, how many economists who got their PhDs before the 70s

221
00:09:31,800 --> 00:09:34,760
are still around?

222
00:09:34,760 --> 00:09:35,440
Walter Williams.

223
00:09:35,440 --> 00:09:36,320
Yes, Walter Williams.

224
00:09:36,320 --> 00:09:48,360
Be happy about all the economists that have reached

225
00:09:48,360 --> 00:09:49,360
these ripe old ages.

226
00:09:49,360 --> 00:09:52,000
Every economist is guaranteed to live to at least 90.

227
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:55,480
This is what we are promised by becoming economists,

228
00:09:55,480 --> 00:09:57,920
to be in good health until at least 90.

229
00:09:57,920 --> 00:10:02,840
And we live to be like Coase, we live to 102 or 103.

230
00:10:02,840 --> 00:10:06,560
So a few years ago, Coase was still rolling around.

231
00:10:06,560 --> 00:10:09,680
And he was like, that guy had multiple successful papers

232
00:10:09,680 --> 00:10:11,080
in the 30s.

233
00:10:11,080 --> 00:10:13,280
And he's alive.

234
00:10:13,280 --> 00:10:14,480
And he's talking to us.

235
00:10:14,480 --> 00:10:15,800
Wow.

236
00:10:15,800 --> 00:10:17,320
All right.

237
00:10:17,320 --> 00:10:19,000
This is all causal, by the way.

238
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:20,560
This is proven to be causal.

239
00:10:20,560 --> 00:10:24,560
Study economics will make you live to these great old ages

240
00:10:24,560 --> 00:10:29,320
with your health, both physical and mental, totally fine.

241
00:10:29,320 --> 00:10:31,560
Chosen right.

242
00:10:31,560 --> 00:10:32,880
All right, anyway.

243
00:10:32,880 --> 00:10:35,560
So the number of people who use the word in this way

244
00:10:35,560 --> 00:10:40,640
is dwindling, but nevertheless still some out there.

245
00:10:40,640 --> 00:10:42,480
Now, before we start going over the evidence,

246
00:10:42,480 --> 00:10:44,040
it's going to be worth pre-answering

247
00:10:44,040 --> 00:10:45,200
some common objections.

248
00:10:47,920 --> 00:10:53,720
So one objection says, look, all theories are false.

249
00:10:53,720 --> 00:10:55,560
So don't go and tell me my theory's false.

250
00:10:55,560 --> 00:11:00,280
What matters is prediction.

251
00:11:00,280 --> 00:11:03,000
Now, this, by the way, is one of the stranger pieces

252
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:05,280
of philosophy that economists have picked up from readings

253
00:11:05,280 --> 00:11:06,360
philosophy of science.

254
00:11:06,360 --> 00:11:08,080
All theories are false.

255
00:11:08,080 --> 00:11:11,320
So how many decimal places?

256
00:11:11,320 --> 00:11:15,400
Are some theories true to one decimal place?

257
00:11:15,400 --> 00:11:17,560
And again, when you think about more,

258
00:11:17,560 --> 00:11:20,040
it's like you go and ask a farmer how far is the town.

259
00:11:20,040 --> 00:11:21,160
He says a mile.

260
00:11:21,160 --> 00:11:24,360
And then you drive there, and it turns out that it's 0.98 miles.

261
00:11:24,360 --> 00:11:26,040
And you say, the farmer lied.

262
00:11:26,040 --> 00:11:28,120
Or the farmer was wrong.

263
00:11:28,120 --> 00:11:29,480
Did he?

264
00:11:29,480 --> 00:11:32,520
Or did he just round according to normal conventions

265
00:11:32,520 --> 00:11:33,960
and what he told you is, in fact,

266
